          "id": "shell-comments",
          "title": "Shell Comments",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 2 Concept 19",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Shell Comments: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Shell Comments\n\nComments are ignored during execution.\n\nA comment begins with `#`.\n\n```sh\n# This is a comment\necho \"Hello\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nIn shell scripting, a **comment** is a textual annotation embedded within a script that is **ignored during execution** by the shell interpreter. A comment begins with the **`#`** character. The shell treats everything from the `#` symbol to the end of that line as non-executable text, meaning it is parsed but produces no command execution or output.\n\nComments serve as documentation for the human reader — explaining intent, marking sections, or temporarily disabling code — while having no effect on the runtime behavior of the script.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe shell processes a script line by line. When it encounters the `#` character at the start of a line (or after whitespace), it recognizes the remainder of that line as a comment and skips execution of that text.\n\n```sh\n# This is a comment\necho \"Hello\"\n```\n\nIn the example above:\n\n1. The shell reads the first line, sees `#`, and **ignores** the entire line.\n2. The shell reads the second line, recognizes `echo` as a command, and executes it, printing `Hello`.\n\nBecause comments are ignored during execution, they can be placed freely wherever documentation is needed without altering the script's output.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — A single-line comment\n\n```sh\n# This is a comment\necho \"Hello\"\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nHello\n```\n\nThe comment line produces no output; only the `echo` command runs.\n\n### Example 2 — Comment followed by executable code\n\n```sh\n# Print a greeting\necho \"Hello\"\n```\n\nThe comment documents the purpose of the following command, but the shell only executes `echo \"Hello\"`.\n\n### Example 3 — Multiple comments in a script\n\n```sh\n# This is a comment\n# Another comment\necho \"Hello\"\n```\n\nBoth comment lines are ignored; the script still prints `Hello`.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe behavior of comments can be modeled as an identity operation on the script's executable content. If a script `S` is composed of comment lines `C` and executable lines `E`, then:\n\n$$\n\\text{Execute}(S) = \\text{Execute}(E)\n$$\n\nThat is, the comment portion `C` contributes nothing to execution:\n\n$$\n\\text{Execute}(C \\cup E) = \\text{Execute}(E)\n$$\n\nEquivalently, for any comment line `c` beginning with `#`:\n\n$$\n\\text{Output}(c) = \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nwhere `ε` denotes the empty output. This formalizes the rule that **comments are ignored during execution**.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Comment (`# ...`) | Executable Command (`echo \"Hello\"`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Starting symbol | `#` | Command name (e.g., `echo`) |\n| Execution effect | Ignored during execution | Executed by the shell |\n| Produces output | No | Yes (e.g., `Hello`) |\n| Purpose | Documentation / annotation | Perform an action |\n| Example | `# This is a comment` | `echo \"Hello\"` |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Expecting comments to run.** A comment such as `# This is a comment` is ignored during execution and will never produce output.\n- **Forgetting the `#`.** Without the leading `#`, text is treated as a command rather than a comment.\n- **Assuming comments affect output.** As shown in the examples, adding or removing comment lines does not change what the script prints.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the core rule: **comments are ignored during execution**.\n- Memorize the marker: **a comment begins with `#`**.\n- Be able to identify which lines in a snippet execute and which do not:\n\n```sh\n# This is a comment\necho \"Hello\"\n```\n\nHere, only `echo \"Hello\"` executes; the comment line is skipped.\n\n- When asked for the output of a script containing comments, ignore every line beginning with `#` and evaluate only the remaining commands.",
