          "id": "pwd",
          "title": "PWD",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 2 Concept 25",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on PWD: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## PWD\n\nPWD normally contains the current working directory.\n\n```sh\necho $PWD\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\n/home/user/project\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**PWD** (Print Working Directory) is a special **shell environment variable** that stores the absolute pathname of the **current working directory** of the running shell session. It is maintained automatically by the shell and reflects the directory in which the user is presently operating. The value of `PWD` is a string representing a fully qualified path, beginning from the root directory (`/`) and ending at the current location.\n\nIn POSIX-compliant shells, `PWD` is a standard environment variable whose value is set and updated by the shell whenever the working directory changes (for example, via the `cd` command). It provides a convenient, script-accessible reference to the present location without needing to invoke an external utility.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe shell tracks the current working directory internally. Whenever the directory changes, the shell updates the `PWD` environment variable to reflect the new absolute path. This variable is then exported to child processes, allowing scripts and programs to read the current directory directly.\n\nKey operational points:\n\n- **Automatic maintenance:** The shell sets `PWD` on startup and updates it after every successful directory change.\n- **Read-only in practice:** Users typically read `PWD` rather than assign to it, since the shell manages its value.\n- **Export to child processes:** Because `PWD` is an environment variable, it is inherited by subshells and executed programs.\n- **Relationship to `pwd` command:** The built-in `pwd` command prints the value of `PWD` (or computes the current directory), while `$PWD` accesses the same information as a variable.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Displaying the current working directory\n\n```sh\necho $PWD\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n/home/user/project\n```\n\nThis prints the absolute path of the directory the shell is currently in.\n\n### Example 2: Using `PWD` in a script\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\necho \"You are currently in: $PWD\"\n```\n\nWhen executed, the script reports the directory from which it was launched (or the directory inherited from the parent shell).\n\n### Example 3: Changing directory and observing the update\n\n```sh\ncd /tmp\necho $PWD\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n/tmp\n```\n\nAfter `cd /tmp`, the shell updates `PWD` to `/tmp`, demonstrating automatic maintenance.\n\n### Example 4: Comparing with the `pwd` command\n\n```sh\npwd\necho $PWD\n```\n\nBoth typically produce the same absolute path, since the `pwd` command and the `PWD` variable reflect the same working directory.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe value of `PWD` can be modeled as an **absolute path string** derived from the root:\n\n$$\n\\text{PWD} = \\text{\"/\"} \\parallel d_1 \\parallel \\text{\"/\"} \\parallel d_2 \\parallel \\dots \\parallel d_n\n$$\n\nwhere $d_1, d_2, \\dots, d_n$ are the successive directory components from the root to the current directory, and $\\parallel$ denotes string concatenation.\n\nEquivalently, if the current directory is reached by a sequence of `cd` operations, then:\n\n$$\n\\text{PWD}_{\\text{new}} = \\text{resolve}(\\text{PWD}_{\\text{old}}, \\text{target})\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{resolve}$ computes the absolute path resulting from applying a directory change to the previous working directory.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | `$PWD` (variable) | `pwd` (command) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Type | Environment variable | Shell built-in / utility |\n| Access | `echo $PWD` | `pwd` |\n| Maintained by | Shell automatically | Computed on invocation |\n| Usable in scripts | Yes, directly as a variable | Yes, via command substitution |\n| Value source | Stored string | Current directory (may resolve symlinks) |\n| Typical output | Absolute path | Absolute path |\n\n| Aspect | `PWD` | Relative path (`.`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Form | Absolute | Relative |\n| Starts from | Root `/` | Current directory |\n| Example | `/home/user/project` | `.` |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming `PWD` is always writable:** The shell manages `PWD`; manually assigning to it may cause inconsistency with the actual directory.\n- **Confusing `$PWD` with `pwd`:** `$PWD` is a variable expansion, while `pwd` is a command; forgetting the `$` yields the literal string `PWD`.\n- **Expecting relative paths:** `PWD` holds an absolute path, not a relative one.\n- **Ignoring symlink resolution differences:** The `pwd` command may resolve symbolic links differently than the stored `PWD` value in some shells.\n- **Forgetting quoting:** Using `$PWD` unquoted in scripts can break when paths contain spaces.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember that **PWD stands for Print Working Directory** and stores the **current working directory**.\n- The canonical way to display it is `echo $PWD`.\n- A typical value looks like `/home/user/project` — an **absolute path**.\n- Distinguish between the **variable** `$PWD` and the **command** `pwd`.\n- Note that the shell **automatically updates** `PWD` whenever the directory changes.\n- Since `PWD` is an **environment variable**, it is inherited by child processes and subshells.\n- Be prepared to state that `PWD` begins at the **root (`/`)** and lists directory components down to the current location.",
