          "id": "if-statement",
          "title": "if Statement",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 2 Concept 40",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on if Statement: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## if Statement\n\n### Academic Definition\n\nThe **`if` statement** in shell scripting is a **conditional control-flow construct** that evaluates a test condition and executes a block of commands only when that condition evaluates to true (i.e., returns a zero exit status). It is the fundamental branching mechanism in Bash and other POSIX-compatible shells, enabling scripts to make decisions based on variable values, command outcomes, or file states.\n\nIn shell semantics, \"true\" means the tested expression exits with status `0`, while \"false\" means a non-zero exit status. This is the inverse of many programming languages, where `0` typically denotes false.\n\n### Working Principle\n\n1. The shell encounters the `if` keyword and evaluates the **condition** that follows.\n2. The condition is tested using `[ ... ]`, which is commonly the shell test command syntax.\n3. If the test returns a **success (exit status 0)**, the commands between `then` and `fi` are executed.\n4. If the test returns a **failure (non-zero exit status)**, the block is skipped and control passes to the statement after `fi`.\n5. The keyword `fi` (if spelled backwards) marks the **end** of the conditional block.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n#### Example 1 — Basic numeric comparison (from notes)\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\n\nage=21\n\nif [ $age -ge 18 ]\nthen\n    echo \"Adult\"\nfi\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nAdult\n```\n\nHere, `$age` expands to `21`, and the test `[ 21 -ge 18 ]` succeeds, so `echo \"Adult\"` runs.\n\n#### Example 2 — Condition false\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\n\nage=15\n\nif [ $age -ge 18 ]\nthen\n    echo \"Adult\"\nfi\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n(no output)\n```\n\nBecause `[ 15 -ge 18 ]` fails, the block is skipped entirely.\n\n#### Example 3 — Using `then` on the same line\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\n\nage=21\n\nif [ $age -ge 18 ]; then\n    echo \"Adult\"\nfi\n```\n\nThe semicolon `;` separates the test from `then`, producing the same output: `Adult`.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe `if` construct can be expressed logically as a conditional function:\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{execute block}, & \\text{if } C(x) = \\text{true (exit 0)} \\\\\n\\text{skip block}, & \\text{if } C(x) = \\text{false (exit} \\neq 0\\text{)}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWhere $C(x)$ is the test condition, e.g., $C(\\text{age}) = (\\text{age} \\ge 18)$.\n\nFor the example: $\\text{age} = 21$, so $C = (21 \\ge 18) = \\text{true}$, therefore the block executes.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | `if` Statement | Alternative Constructs |\n|--------|----------------|------------------------|\n| Purpose | Conditional execution | Loops (`for`, `while`) repeat execution |\n| Termination keyword | `fi` | `done` (loops), `esac` (case) |\n| Condition syntax | `[ ... ]` test command | `[[ ... ]]`, `(( ... ))`, `test` |\n| Truth convention | Exit status `0` = true | Same across shell constructs |\n| Multi-branch | Requires `elif` / `else` | `case` handles pattern matching |\n\n| Test Operator | Meaning | Example |\n|---------------|---------|---------|\n| `-ge` | Greater than or equal | `[ $age -ge 18 ]` |\n| `-gt` | Greater than | `[ $age -gt 18 ]` |\n| `-le` | Less than or equal | `[ $age -le 18 ]` |\n| `-lt` | Less than | `[ $age -lt 18 ]` |\n| `-eq` | Equal | `[ $age -eq 18 ]` |\n| `-ne` | Not equal | `[ $age -ne 18 ]` |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting `fi`**: Every `if` must be closed with `fi`, otherwise the script produces a syntax error.\n- **Omitting spaces inside `[ ]`**: `[$age -ge 18]` fails; the correct form is `[ $age -ge 18 ]` with spaces around brackets.\n- **Confusing `=` and `-eq`**: Use `-eq` for numeric comparison and `=` for string comparison.\n- **Assuming `0` means false**: In shell, exit status `0` means **true/success**.\n- **Missing `then`**: The `then` keyword is mandatory (either on a new line or after `;`).\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the **exit-status convention**: `0` = true, non-zero = false.\n- Memorize the closing keyword **`fi`** — it is unique to the `if` construct.\n- Know that `[ ... ]` is the **test command syntax** and requires surrounding spaces.\n- Be able to write the canonical example from memory:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\n\nage=21\n\nif [ $age -ge 18 ]\nthen\n    echo \"Adult\"\nfi\n```\n\n- Practice predicting output: if the test fails, **no output** is produced.\n- Distinguish numeric operators (`-ge`, `-gt`, `-le`, `-lt`, `-eq`, `-ne`) from string operators (`=`, `!=`).",
