          "id": "case-statement",
          "title": "case Statement",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 2 Concept 47",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on case Statement: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## case Statement\n\n### Academic Definition\n\nThe **`case` statement** in shell scripting is a **multi-way branching control structure** that compares a single value (typically a variable) against a list of **patterns**. It is the shell's equivalent of the `switch`/`case` construct found in languages such as C, Java, or Python. Instead of evaluating a long chain of `if`/`elif`/`else` conditions, `case` selects exactly one matching branch and executes the commands associated with it. When no pattern matches, an optional **default branch** (commonly written as `*`) is executed.\n\nThe construct is terminated by the keyword **`esac`** (which is simply `case` spelled backwards), a naming convention shared with `if ... fi` in shell syntax.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe `case` statement operates on a **pattern-matching** basis rather than a strict equality test:\n\n1. The shell evaluates the **word** (usually `$variable`) once.\n2. It compares this word against each **pattern** in order, from top to bottom.\n3. The **first** pattern that matches is selected; its command block runs.\n4. Execution then jumps to **after `esac`** — no fall-through occurs (unlike C's `switch` without `break`).\n5. Each branch is terminated by the **`;;`** operator, which marks the end of that branch's command list.\n6. If no pattern matches, the **`*)`** default branch runs (if present).\n\nBecause patterns are matched in order, the **order of patterns matters** — a broad pattern placed early can shadow a more specific pattern placed later.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```sh\ncase $variable in\n    pattern1)\n        commands\n        ;;\n    pattern2)\n        commands\n        ;;\n    *)\n        default commands\n        ;;\nesac\n```\n\n### Detailed Example\n\n```sh\nread choice\n\ncase $choice in\n    1)\n        echo \"Add\"\n        ;;\n    2)\n        echo \"Delete\"\n        ;;\n    3)\n        echo \"Exit\"\n        ;;\n    *)\n        echo \"Invalid choice\"\n        ;;\nesac\n```\n\nIn this example, the user's input stored in `choice` is compared against the literal patterns `1`, `2`, and `3`. Any value that does not equal one of these falls through to the `*)` branch, which prints `Invalid choice`.\n\n### Flow of Control\n\n```text\nchoice\n                       │\n          ┌────────────┼────────────┐\n          ▼            ▼            ▼\n          1            2            3\n          │            │            │\n          ▼            ▼            ▼\n         Add         Delete        Exit\n                       │\n                 Other values\n                       │\n                       ▼\n                    Invalid\n```\n\nThe diagram shows the single evaluated value (`choice`) fanning out into mutually exclusive branches. Exactly one branch is taken: `1 → Add`, `2 → Delete`, `3 → Exit`, and everything else → `Invalid`.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n\nThe `case` construct can be modelled as a piecewise function over the input word \\(x\\):\n\n$$\nf(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{Add}, & x = 1 \\\\\n\\text{Delete}, & x = 2 \\\\\n\\text{Exit}, & x = 3 \\\\\n\\text{Invalid}, & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nMore generally, for patterns \\(p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_n\\) and a default \\(p_{*}\\):\n\n$$\nf(x) = c_k \\quad \\text{where } k = \\min\\{\\, i \\mid x \\text{ matches } p_i \\,\\}\n$$\n\nand if no \\(p_i\\) matches, \\(f(x) = c_{*}\\) (the default branch). This formalises the **first-match-wins** semantics of `case`.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | `case` Statement | `if`/`elif`/`else` Chain |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | Compare one value against many patterns | Evaluate arbitrary boolean conditions |\n| Matching | Pattern-based (supports globs) | Condition-based (test expressions) |\n| Readability | High for many discrete options | Degrades with many branches |\n| Branch terminator | `;;` | `fi` (single closing keyword) |\n| Default branch | `*)` | `else` |\n| Fall-through | None (first match wins) | Not applicable |\n| Typical use | Menus, command dispatch | Range/logic checks |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting `;;`** at the end of a branch, which causes commands to run together or produces syntax errors.\n- **Omitting `esac`**, leaving the construct unterminated.\n- **Placing the default `*)` branch too early**, which shadows all later patterns.\n- **Assuming fall-through** as in C — shell `case` stops after the first match.\n- **Quoting issues** — patterns are matched literally/glob-wise, so unintended glob characters can match more than expected.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Memorise the **skeleton**: `case ... in ... ;; ... esac`.\n- Remember the terminator is **`;;`**, not `break`.\n- The default branch is written as **`*)`**.\n- Emphasise **first-match-wins** and **no fall-through**.\n- Use the menu example (`Add` / `Delete` / `Exit` / `Invalid`) as a canonical illustration.\n- Note that `esac` is `case` reversed — a common memory aid.",
