          "id": "argc",
          "title": "argc",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 3 Concept 3",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on argc: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## argc\n\n`argc` stores the number of command-line arguments, including the program name.\n\nExample:\n\n```sh\n./program one two three\n```\n\nThe values are:\n\n```text\nargc = 4\n\nargv[0] = \"./program\"\nargv[1] = \"one\"\nargv[2] = \"two\"\nargv[3] = \"three\"\n```\n\nDiagram:\n\n```text\nargc = 4\n                  │\n        ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n        ▼         ▼         ▼         ▼\n      argv[0]   argv[1]   argv[2]   argv[3]\n        │         │         │         │\n    program       one       two      three\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**`argc`** (short for **argument count**) is a formal parameter of the `main` function in C and C++ programs. It is an integer value that represents the **total number of command-line arguments** passed to the program at the time of execution, **including the program name itself**.\n\nThe standard signature of `main` that accepts command-line arguments is:\n\n```c\nint main(int argc, char *argv[])\n```\n\nor equivalently:\n\n```c\nint main(int argc, char **argv)\n```\n\nHere:\n- **`argc`** — argument count (an `int`)\n- **`argv`** — argument vector (an array of strings / array of `char *`)\n\n`argc` is guaranteed by the C standard to be **non-negative**. If no arguments are supplied beyond the program name, `argc` equals **1**.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nWhen a program is invoked from a shell or command line, the operating system and the C runtime startup code perform the following steps:\n\n1. **Tokenization** — The shell splits the command line into individual tokens separated by whitespace (spaces, tabs).\n2. **Passing to the runtime** — These tokens are passed to the program's entry point.\n3. **Counting** — The runtime counts the tokens, including the program name, and stores this count in `argc`.\n4. **Populating `argv`** — Each token is stored as a null-terminated string in `argv[0]`, `argv[1]`, … `argv[argc-1]`.\n5. **Termination marker** — `argv[argc]` is guaranteed to be a **NULL pointer**, marking the end of the argument list.\n\nThe relationship can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{argc} = \\text{number of tokens on the command line (including program name)}\n$$\n\nAnd the valid indices of `argv` are:\n\n$$\n0 \\leq i \\leq \\text{argc} - 1\n$$\n\nwith `argv[argc] == NULL`.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — No arguments\n\n```sh\n./program\n```\n\n```text\nargc = 1\nargv[0] = \"./program\"\nargv[1] = NULL\n```\n\n### Example 2 — One argument\n\n```sh\n./program hello\n```\n\n```text\nargc = 2\nargv[0] = \"./program\"\nargv[1] = \"hello\"\nargv[2] = NULL\n```\n\n### Example 3 — Three arguments (from original notes)\n\n```sh\n./program one two three\n```\n\n```text\nargc = 4\nargv[0] = \"./program\"\nargv[1] = \"one\"\nargv[2] = \"two\"\nargv[3] = \"three\"\nargv[4] = NULL\n```\n\n### Example 4 — Using `argc` in code\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    printf(\"argc = %d\\n\", argc);\n    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {\n        printf(\"argv[%d] = %s\\n\", i, argv[i]);\n    }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nRunning `./program one two three` produces:\n\n```text\nargc = 4\nargv[0] = ./program\nargv[1] = one\nargv[2] = two\nargv[3] = three\n```\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet $n$ be the number of user-supplied arguments (excluding the program name). Then:\n\n$$\n\\text{argc} = n + 1\n$$\n\nThe valid range of indices for `argv` is:\n\n$$\ni \\in \\{0, 1, 2, \\dots, \\text{argc} - 1\\}\n$$\n\nThe sentinel value satisfies:\n\n$$\n\\text{argv}[\\text{argc}] = \\text{NULL}\n$$\n\nA common loop bound check:\n\n$$\n\\text{while } (i < \\text{argc}) \\implies \\text{process } \\text{argv}[i]\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | `argc` | `argv` |\n|--------|--------|--------|\n| Full name | Argument count | Argument vector |\n| Type | `int` | `char *[]` (array of strings) |\n| Purpose | Stores the number of arguments | Stores the actual argument strings |\n| Includes program name? | Yes (counts it) | Yes (`argv[0]`) |\n| Value when no args given | `1` | `argv[0]` = program name |\n| Sentinel | None (just an integer) | `argv[argc] == NULL` |\n| Used for | Loop bounds, validation | Reading argument values |\n| Modifiable? | Yes (local copy) | Contents modifiable; pointer array modifiable |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Forgetting that `argc` includes the program name** — Beginners often assume `argc` equals the number of user arguments. It is always **one more**.\n2. **Off-by-one errors in loops** — Using `i <= argc` instead of `i < argc` reads `argv[argc]`, which is `NULL`.\n3. **Assuming `argc` is always ≥ 1** — While the standard guarantees non-negative, in practice `argc` is at least `1` because the program name is always present.\n4. **Not checking `argc` before accessing `argv[i]`** — Accessing `argv[2]` when `argc == 2` is undefined behavior.\n5. **Confusing `argc` with the number of user arguments** — Remember: `user_args = argc - 1`.\n6. **Modifying `argc` and expecting the change to persist** — `argc` is a local parameter; changes do not affect the caller.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Memorize the formula:** `argc = (number of user arguments) + 1`.\n- **Remember the sentinel:** `argv[argc]` is always `NULL` — a favorite exam question.\n- **Know the signature:** `int main(int argc, char *argv[])` — be able to write it from memory.\n- **Trace examples carefully:** Given a command line, be able to list `argc` and every `argv[i]` value.\n- **Watch for off-by-one traps** in loop conditions (`i < argc` is correct).\n- **Distinguish `argc` (count) from `argv` (vector)** — a common MCQ distinction.\n- **Edge case:** `./program` alone gives `argc = 1`, not `0`.\n- **Diagram recall:** The tree diagram from the notes (argc branching into `argv[0]`…`argv[argc-1]`) is a strong visual aid for exams.",
