          "id": "system-v-message-queues",
          "title": "System V Message Queues",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 3 Concept 18",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on System V Message Queues: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## System V Message Queues\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**System V Message Queues** are one of the three original **System V Inter-Process Communication (IPC)** mechanisms (alongside shared memory and semaphores). A message queue is a **kernel-managed, linked-list-based data structure** that allows processes to exchange data in the form of **discrete messages**. Each message is associated with a **type** (a positive long integer), enabling **type-based selective retrieval** rather than strict FIFO ordering. The queue persists in kernel space independently of the processes that use it, allowing asynchronous communication between unrelated processes.\n\nCommon System V message queue functions:\n\n```text\nmsgget()   → Create/access queue\nmsgsnd()   → Send message\nmsgrcv()   → Receive message\nmsgctl()   → Control/remove queue\n```\n\nHeaders commonly include:\n\n```c\n#include <sys/types.h>\n#include <sys/ipc.h>\n#include <sys/msg.h>\n```\n\nBasic flow:\n\n```text\nmsgget()\n                │\n                ▼\n          Message Queue\n             /      \\\n            /        \\\n       msgsnd()     msgrcv()\n          │            │\n          ▼            ▼\n      Sender         Receiver\n```\n\n---\n\n### Working Principle\n\n1. **Queue Creation / Access** — A process calls `msgget()` with a **key** (often generated via `ftok()`) and a set of flags. If the queue does not exist and `IPC_CREAT` is specified, the kernel creates it and returns a **queue identifier (msqid)**. If it already exists, the identifier is returned.\n2. **Message Sending** — A sender calls `msgsnd()` with the `msqid`, a pointer to a message buffer, its size, and flags. The kernel appends the message to the queue's internal linked list.\n3. **Message Receiving** — A receiver calls `msgrcv()` with the `msqid`, a buffer, the desired size, a **message type selector**, and flags. The kernel scans the queue and returns the first matching message.\n4. **Control / Removal** — `msgctl()` performs operations such as retrieving status (`IPC_STAT`), setting attributes (`IPC_SET`), or removing the queue (`IPC_RMID`).\n\nThe kernel maintains the queue across process lifetimes, so senders and receivers need not run simultaneously.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Message buffer structure** (conventional form):\n\n```c\nstruct msgbuf {\n    long mtype;       /* message type, must be > 0 */\n    char mtext[100];  /* message data */\n};\n```\n\n**Sender process:**\n\n```c\nkey_t key = ftok(\"progfile\", 65);\nint msqid = msgget(key, 0666 | IPC_CREAT);\nstruct msgbuf msg;\nmsg.mtype = 1;\nstrcpy(msg.mtext, \"Hello Receiver\");\nmsgsnd(msqid, &msg, sizeof(msg.mtext), 0);\n```\n\n**Receiver process:**\n\n```c\nkey_t key = ftok(\"progfile\", 65);\nint msqid = msgget(key, 0666 | IPC_CREAT);\nstruct msgbuf msg;\nmsgrcv(msqid, &msg, sizeof(msg.mtext), 1, 0);\nprintf(\"Received: %s\\n\", msg.mtext);\n```\n\n**Removing the queue:**\n\n```c\nmsgctl(msqid, IPC_RMID, NULL);\n```\n\n---\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe **message type selector** in `msgrcv()` follows these rules:\n\n- If `msgtyp == 0` → return the **first message** in the queue (FIFO).\n- If `msgtyp > 0` → return the **first message of that exact type**.\n- If `msgtyp < 0` → return the **first message whose type is ≤ |msgtyp|** (lowest type first).\n\nFormally, for a queue $Q = \\{m_1, m_2, \\dots, m_n\\}$ with types $t_i$:\n\n$$\n\\text{msgrcv}(Q, \\tau) =\n\\begin{cases}\nm_1 & \\tau = 0 \\\\\n\\min\\{m_i : t_i = \\tau\\} & \\tau > 0 \\\\\n\\min\\{m_i : t_i \\le |\\tau|\\} & \\tau < 0\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe **maximum message size** is bounded by the kernel constant `MSGMAX`, and the **maximum total queue size** by `MSGMNB`.\n\n---\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | System V Message Queue | POSIX Message Queue | Pipe |\n|---------|------------------------|---------------------|------|\n| API style | `msgget/msgsnd/msgrcv/msgctl` | `mq_open/mq_send/mq_receive` | `pipe()` |\n| Message boundaries | Preserved | Preserved | Byte stream |\n| Type-based selection | Yes (`mtype`) | Priority-based | No |\n| Persistence | Kernel-persistent | Kernel-persistent | Process-lifetime |\n| Naming | Key via `ftok()` | Name string | File descriptors |\n\n---\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting `mtype > 0`** — message types must be strictly positive; zero or negative types cause errors.\n- **Mismatched size in `msgsnd`/`msgrcv`** — the size argument must exclude the `mtype` field.\n- **Not removing the queue** — queues persist after process exit; use `msgctl(..., IPC_RMID, ...)`.\n- **Ignoring return values** — `msgget`, `msgsnd`, `msgrcv`, and `msgctl` all return `-1` on error.\n- **Assuming FIFO** — retrieval depends on the type selector, not just arrival order.\n\n---\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the **four core functions** and their roles: `msgget`, `msgsnd`, `msgrcv`, `msgctl`.\n- Remember the **three required headers**: `<sys/types.h>`, `<sys/ipc.h>`, `<sys/msg.h>`.\n- Be ready to explain the **three `msgtyp` cases** (0, >0, <0) in `msgrcv()`.\n- Know the **message buffer layout**: `long mtype` first, then the data array.\n- Distinguish **System V** vs **POSIX** message queues in comparison questions.\n- Recall that queues are **kernel-persistent** and require explicit removal via `IPC_RMID`.",
