          "id": "accept",
          "title": "accept()",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 3 Concept 43",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on accept(): definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## accept()\n\n`accept()` accepts a pending client connection.\n\n```c\nint newfd = accept(sockfd, NULL, NULL);\n```\n\nThe listening socket normally continues to listen while the returned socket is used for communication with the accepted client.\n\n```text\nListening Socket\n       │\n       ▼\n   accept()\n       │\n       ▼\nConnected Socket\n       │\n       ▼\nsend()/recv()\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n`accept()` is a **blocking system call** in the Berkeley Sockets API used by a **server** to extract the first connection request from the queue of pending connections on a **listening socket**. It creates a **new connected socket** that is used for communication with the accepted client, while the original listening socket remains open and continues to accept further connections.\n\nFormally, `accept()` dequeues a completed connection from the **accept queue** (also called the completed connection queue) associated with the listening socket `sockfd`, and returns a new file descriptor referring to that connection.\n\n### Prototype\n\n```c\n#include <sys/socket.h>\n\nint accept(int sockfd, struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t *addrlen);\n```\n\n- `sockfd` — a socket that has been created with `socket()`, bound with `bind()`, and marked as passive with `listen()`.\n- `addr` — optional pointer to a `sockaddr` structure that receives the **peer (client) address**.\n- `addrlen` — value-result parameter; on input the size of the buffer pointed to by `addr`, on output the actual size of the peer address.\n- **Return value** — a new non-negative file descriptor on success; `-1` on error.\n\nIn the given example, `NULL, NULL` are passed for `addr` and `addrlen`, meaning the server does not need the client's address.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. The server creates a socket with `socket()`.\n2. The socket is bound to a local address with `bind()`.\n3. `listen()` marks the socket as **passive/listening** and creates the pending-connection queue.\n4. A client calls `connect()`; the kernel completes the TCP three-way handshake and places the connection in the **accept queue**.\n5. The server calls `accept()`, which **blocks** if the queue is empty.\n6. When a connection is available, `accept()` returns a **new file descriptor** for that specific connection.\n7. The **listening socket** (`sockfd`) remains open and continues to listen for new clients.\n8. The server uses the returned **connected socket** (`newfd`) for `send()`/`recv()`.\n\n```text\nListening Socket (sockfd)\n       │\n       ▼\n   accept()  ── blocks until a client connects\n       │\n       ▼\nConnected Socket (newfd)  ── per-connection\n       │\n       ▼\nsend()/recv()\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Minimal server loop\n\n```c\nint sockfd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);\nbind(sockfd, ...);\nlisten(sockfd, BACKLOG);\n\nwhile (1) {\n    int newfd = accept(sockfd, NULL, NULL);\n    if (newfd < 0) {\n        /* handle error */\n        continue;\n    }\n    /* communicate with the client on newfd */\n    send(newfd, buf, len, 0);\n    recv(newfd, buf, len, 0);\n    close(newfd);\n}\n```\n\n### Capturing the client address\n\n```c\nstruct sockaddr_in cli;\nsocklen_t clilen = sizeof(cli);\nint newfd = accept(sockfd, (struct sockaddr *)&cli, &clilen);\n```\n\nHere `cli` is filled with the client's address and `clilen` is updated to the actual size.\n\n### Key observations\n\n- The **listening socket** is reused across iterations.\n- The **connected socket** is unique per client and must be `close()`d when done.\n- If `accept()` fails, the listening socket is unaffected.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the listening socket be \\( L \\) and the set of accepted connections be \\( C = \\{c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_n\\} \\).\n\n- Each call to `accept(L, ...)` returns a distinct \\( c_i \\).\n- The listening socket satisfies: \\( L \\notin C \\), i.e., the listening socket is **never** used for data transfer.\n- The number of pending connections is bounded by the **backlog** parameter \\( B \\) passed to `listen()`:\n\n\\[\n0 \\le |\\text{accept queue}| \\le B\n\\]\n\n- If the queue is full, further connection attempts may be refused or dropped depending on the implementation.\n- `accept()` is a **blocking** operation: if the queue is empty, the calling thread sleeps until a connection arrives (unless the socket is non-blocking or `O_NONBLOCK` is set).\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Listening Socket (`sockfd`) | Connected Socket (`newfd`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Created by | `socket()` | `accept()` |\n| Purpose | Accept new connections | Communicate with one client |\n| State | Passive / listening | Connected / established |\n| Used for `send()`/`recv()` | No | Yes |\n| Lifetime | Entire server lifetime | Until client disconnects |\n| Reused across clients | Yes | No (one per client) |\n| Returned by `accept()` | No | Yes |\n\n| Function | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `socket()` | Create an endpoint |\n| `bind()` | Attach a local address |\n| `listen()` | Mark as passive, set backlog |\n| `accept()` | Dequeue a pending connection |\n| `connect()` | Client-side: initiate connection |\n| `send()`/`recv()` | Data transfer on connected socket |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Using the listening socket for data transfer.** `send()`/`recv()` must be called on the socket returned by `accept()`, not on `sockfd`.\n2. **Forgetting to `close()` the connected socket.** This leaks file descriptors.\n3. **Calling `accept()` on a socket that was not `listen()`ed.** It will fail.\n4. **Ignoring the return value.** A return of `-1` indicates an error and must be handled.\n5. **Assuming `accept()` returns the same socket.** Each call returns a **new** descriptor.\n6. **Passing an uninitialized `addrlen`.** It must be set to `sizeof(addr)` before the call.\n7. **Blocking indefinitely in single-threaded servers.** A slow client can stall the loop; use non-blocking I/O or multiple threads/processes.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the **two-socket model**: one listening socket, one connected socket per client.\n- Be able to write the **canonical server sequence**: `socket → bind → listen → accept → send/recv → close`.\n- Know that `accept()` **blocks** by default and returns a **new file descriptor**.\n- Distinguish clearly between the **listening socket** and the **connected socket** — this is a common exam question.\n- Recall the prototype: `int accept(int sockfd, struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t *addrlen);`\n- `NULL, NULL` for `addr`/`addrlen` means the client address is not required.\n- The listening socket **continues to listen** after `accept()` returns — it is not consumed.",
