          "id": "unix-file-system",
          "title": "UNIX File System",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 4 Concept 2",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on UNIX File System: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## UNIX File System\n\nThe UNIX file system organizes data in a hierarchical tree structure.\n\nThe top-level directory is called the root directory, represented by `/`.\n\n```text\n/\n                         │\n       ┌─────────────────┼──────────────────┐\n       ▼                 ▼                  ▼\n     /bin              /home              /etc\n       │                 │                  │\n       │          ┌──────┴──────┐           │\n       │          ▼             ▼           │\n       │       user1          user2         │\n       │          │             │           │\n       │       files         files          │\n       │\n       ├── /dev\n       ├── /tmp\n       ├── /usr\n       ├── /var\n       └── /mnt\n```\n\nImportant directories:\n\n| Directory | Purpose |\n| --------- | ------- |\n| `/` | Root of the file system |\n| `/bin` | Essential executable commands |\n| `/sbin` | System administration commands |\n| `/etc` | System configuration files |\n| `/home` | Users' home directories |\n| `/dev` | Device files |\n| `/tmp` | Temporary files |\n| `/usr` | User applications and libraries |\n| `/var` | Variable data such as logs |\n| `/mnt` | Common mount point |\n| `/boot` | Boot-related files |\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **UNIX file system** is a hierarchical, tree-structured organization of data in which all files and directories descend from a single top-level directory called the **root directory**, represented by the forward slash character `/`. Every file, directory, and device in the system is reachable by traversing this single inverted tree, beginning at `/`. The tree is composed of **directories** (which may contain other directories and files) and **files** (leaf nodes holding data). Because the structure is unified under one root, the UNIX file system is described as a **single-rooted hierarchical file system**, in contrast to systems that expose multiple independent drive letters or volumes.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe UNIX file system operates on the principle of **hierarchical containment**:\n\n1. **Single root:** All paths originate at `/`. There is exactly one root directory for the entire file system tree.\n2. **Directories as containers:** A directory is a special node that can hold entries pointing to files or to other directories. This nesting produces the tree.\n3. **Recursive descent:** Any location in the tree is identified by a **path** formed by concatenating directory names separated by `/`, starting from the root.\n4. **Branching:** From the root, the tree branches into major system directories such as `/bin`, `/home`, and `/etc`, each of which may branch further (for example, `/home` contains `user1` and `user2`, each containing their own files).\n5. **Uniform naming:** Because every node lives under `/`, a single naming scheme addresses the entire system, including device files under `/dev` and mount points such as `/mnt`.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Navigating the tree\n\nStarting at the root `/`, the tree branches into `/bin`, `/home`, and `/etc`. The `/home` branch further splits into `user1` and `user2`, each of which contains `files`. This illustrates how a user's data is nested several levels below the root.\n\n### Example 2: System directory layout\n\nThe root also contains additional branches:\n\n```text\n/\n├── /bin\n├── /dev\n├── /tmp\n├── /usr\n├── /var\n└── /mnt\n```\n\nEach of these serves a distinct role:\n\n- `/bin` — Essential executable commands\n- `/dev` — Device files\n- `/tmp` — Temporary files\n- `/usr` — User applications and libraries\n- `/var` — Variable data such as logs\n- `/mnt` — Common mount point\n\n### Example 3: Home directory placement\n\nA user's home directory is located under `/home`. For instance, `user1` and `user2` each have their own directory beneath `/home`, and each of those directories contains that user's `files`. This demonstrates the hierarchical grouping of per-user data.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe file system can be modeled as a **rooted tree** $T = (V, E)$ where:\n\n- $V$ is the set of all nodes (files and directories),\n- $E$ is the set of edges representing containment relationships,\n- The **root** is the unique node $r = \\texttt{/}$ with no parent.\n\nFor any node $v \\in V$, its **path** from the root is the sequence of node names:\n\n$$\n\\text{path}(v) = \\texttt{/} \\; n_1 \\; \\texttt{/} \\; n_2 \\; \\texttt{/} \\cdots \\texttt{/} \\; n_k\n$$\n\nwhere $n_k$ is the name of $v$ and each $n_i$ is an ancestor directory.\n\nThe **depth** of a node is the number of edges on the path from the root:\n\n$$\n\\text{depth}(v) = k\n$$\n\nwith $\\text{depth}(\\texttt{/}) = 0$.\n\nThe tree property guarantees that for every node $v \\neq r$, there is exactly **one** parent, hence exactly one canonical path from the root — a defining characteristic of the single-rooted hierarchy.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | UNIX File System (as described) | Contrasting Model |\n| ------ | ------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| Root | Single root `/` | Multiple independent roots/drives |\n| Structure | Hierarchical tree | Flat or multi-rooted |\n| Addressing | Paths from `/` | Separate per-volume naming |\n| Device access | Device files under `/dev` | Devices addressed outside the file tree |\n| Mounting | Mount points such as `/mnt` | Volumes attached as separate roots |\n| User data | Under `/home` | Often under separate user areas |\n\n| Directory | Purpose |\n| --------- | ------- |\n| `/` | Root of the file system |\n| `/bin` | Essential executable commands |\n| `/sbin` | System administration commands |\n| `/etc` | System configuration files |\n| `/home` | Users' home directories |\n| `/dev` | Device files |\n| `/tmp` | Temporary files |\n| `/usr` | User applications and libraries |\n| `/var` | Variable data such as logs |\n| `/mnt` | Common mount point |\n| `/boot` | Boot-related files |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing `/` with other directories.** The root is `/`; it is the top of the entire tree, not merely another folder.\n- **Assuming multiple roots.** The UNIX file system is single-rooted — everything descends from `/`.\n- **Mixing up directory purposes.** For example, treating `/etc` (configuration files) as a place for user data, or `/tmp` (temporary files) as permanent storage.\n- **Forgetting `/home` holds user directories.** Users' home directories live under `/home`, not at the root level directly.\n- **Overlooking `/dev`.** Device files reside under `/dev`; devices are represented within the file tree.\n- **Misplacing `/var`.** Variable data such as logs belongs under `/var`, not under static system directories.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Memorize the root symbol.** The root directory is represented by `/` — a frequently tested fact.\n- **Know the tree shape.** Be able to reproduce the branching from `/` into `/bin`, `/home`, and `/etc`, with `/home` splitting into `user1` and `user2`, each containing `files`.\n- **Learn the directory table.** The purposes of `/`, `/bin`, `/sbin`, `/etc`, `/home`, `/dev`, `/tmp`, `/usr`, `/var`, `/mnt`, and `/boot` are prime exam material.\n- **Remember the single-root principle.** Emphasize that the UNIX file system is a hierarchical tree with one root.\n- **Use the diagram.** Sketching the tree from `/` downward is an effective way to answer structure-based questions.\n- **Distinguish similar directories.** Keep `/bin` (essential commands) versus `/sbin` (system administration commands), and `/usr` (user applications and libraries) versus `/var` (variable data such as logs) clearly separated in your notes.",
