          "notes": "## File Permissions\n\nUNIX provides permissions to control who can read, write, or execute a file.\n\nThere are three basic permissions:\n\n```text\nr → Read\nw → Write\nx → Execute\n```\n\nThey are applied to three categories:\n\n```text\nu → User/Owner\ng → Group\no → Others\n```\n\nDiagram:\n\n```text\nFile\n                  │\n       ┌──────────┼──────────┐\n       ▼          ▼          ▼\n     User       Group      Others\n       │          │          │\n      rwx        rwx        rwx\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**File Permissions** in UNIX are a fundamental access-control mechanism that determines **who** can perform **which operations** on a file. Every file in a UNIX system carries a permission set that is checked by the kernel whenever a process attempts to access the file.\n\nFormally, a file's permission is a **9-bit field** (plus special bits) organized as three triads:\n\n- **User (u)** — permissions for the file's owner.\n- **Group (g)** — permissions for members of the file's group.\n- **Others (o)** — permissions for everyone else on the system.\n\nEach triad encodes three independent rights: **Read (r)**, **Write (w)**, and **Execute (x)**. This design reflects the classical UNIX philosophy of **discretionary access control (DAC)** — the owner decides who gets access.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nWhen a process requests access to a file, the kernel performs the following steps:\n\n1. **Identify the requester** — determine the effective user ID (UID) and group ID (GID) of the process.\n2. **Match against categories** — check whether the requester is the **owner**, a member of the file's **group**, or falls under **others**.\n3. **Apply the matching triad** — only the triad corresponding to the *first matching category* is consulted (owner is checked before group, group before others).\n4. **Grant or deny** — if the required bit (r, w, or x) is set, access is allowed; otherwise it is denied.\n\nThis ordered evaluation means that if you are the owner, the **owner triad alone** governs your access — the group and others triads are ignored for you.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Reading a permission string\n\n```text\n-rwxr-xr--  1 alice  devs  4096 Jan 1 10:00 report.sh\n```\n\nBreaking the 9-bit field into triads:\n\n| Category | Bits | Meaning |\n|----------|------|---------|\n| User (u) | `rwx` | Owner can read, write, execute |\n| Group (g) | `r-x` | Group can read and execute, **not** write |\n| Others (o) | `r--` | Others can only read |\n\n### Example 2 — Interpreting intent\n\n- `rwx` for **user** → the owner has full control.\n- `r-x` for **group** → collaborators can run and inspect but not modify.\n- `r--` for **others** → the file is publicly readable but protected from modification.\n\n### Example 3 — Category matching\n\nIf user `bob` (a member of group `devs`) tries to **write** to `report.sh`:\n\n- `bob` is **not** the owner (`alice` is), so the owner triad is skipped.\n- `bob` **is** in group `devs`, so the group triad `r-x` applies.\n- The `w` bit is **not** set in the group triad → **write is denied**, even though `bob` might otherwise expect access.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nEach permission triad can be represented as a **3-bit binary number**, where each bit corresponds to a right:\n\n$$\n\\text{Triad} = (r \\cdot 4) + (w \\cdot 2) + (x \\cdot 1)\n$$\n\nwhere each of $r, w, x \\in \\{0, 1\\}$.\n\nThe full permission field is the concatenation of the three triads:\n\n$$\n\\text{Permission} = (\\text{User} \\times 8^2) + (\\text{Group} \\times 8^1) + (\\text{Others} \\times 8^0)\n$$\n\nThis is why permissions are conventionally written in **octal**:\n\n| Symbolic | Binary (u g o) | Octal |\n|----------|----------------|-------|\n| `rwx` | 111 | 7 |\n| `rw-` | 110 | 6 |\n| `r-x` | 101 | 5 |\n| `r--` | 100 | 4 |\n| `-wx` | 011 | 3 |\n| `-w-` | 010 | 2 |\n| `--x` | 001 | 1 |\n| `---` | 000 | 0 |\n\nThus `rwxr-xr--` = `7 5 4` = **754** in octal.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | User (u) | Group (g) | Others (o) |\n|--------|----------|-----------|------------|\n| **Applies to** | File owner | Members of file's group | Everyone else |\n| **Checked when** | Requester is owner | Requester is in group (and not owner) | Requester is neither owner nor group member |\n| **Typical intent** | Full control | Collaboration | Public/limited access |\n| **Priority** | Highest | Middle | Lowest |\n| **Example bits** | `rwx` | `r-x` | `r--` |\n\n| Permission | Symbol | Grants ability to |\n|------------|--------|-------------------|\n| Read | `r` | View file contents |\n| Write | `w` | Modify file contents |\n| Execute | `x` | Run file as a program |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Assuming all three triads apply** — only the *first matching category* is used. If you are the owner, group and others bits are irrelevant to you.\n2. **Confusing category order** — the kernel checks **user → group → others**, not the reverse.\n3. **Forgetting that `x` means different things** — for a directory, `x` controls traversal, not execution of code.\n4. **Mixing up octal and symbolic notation** — `754` and `rwxr-xr--` describe the same thing; converting incorrectly leads to wrong permissions.\n5. **Believing group membership grants owner rights** — a group member never inherits the owner triad.\n6. **Overlooking the leading character** — the first character (`-`, `d`, `l`) indicates file type, not permission.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Memorize the octal table** (`r=4`, `w=2`, `x=1`) — it is the fastest way to answer permission questions.\n- **Remember the triad order**: `u`, then `g`, then `o` — both in symbolic strings and octal digits.\n- **Practice converting** between symbolic (`rwxr-xr--`) and octal (`754`) forms; this is a very common exam question.\n- **Recall the matching rule**: owner is checked first, then group, then others — only one triad applies per access attempt.\n- **Draw the diagram** (File → User/Group/Others → rwx) to quickly reason about any permission scenario.\n- **Watch for the leading file-type character** when reading a permission string — it is not part of the 9-bit permission field.",
