          "id": "permission-representation",
          "title": "Permission Representation",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 4 Concept 11",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Permission Representation: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Permission Representation\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Permission Representation** in Unix-like operating systems is the symbolic notation used to describe the access rights granted to three distinct classes of users — the **Owner**, the **Group**, and **Others** — over a file or directory. Each file carries a set of permission bits that determine whether a given class may **read (r)**, **write (w)**, or **execute (x)** the file. The symbolic string is a human-readable rendering of these bits, prefixed by a single character that identifies the **File type**.\n\nConsider:\n\n```text\n-rwxr-xr--\n```\n\nBreakdown:\n\n```text\n- | rwx | r-x | r--\n  │   │     │     │\n  │   │     │     └── Others\n  │   │     └──────── Group\n  │   └────────────── Owner\n  └────────────────── File type\n```\n\nMeaning:\n\n```text\nOwner  → read + write + execute\nGroup  → read + execute\nOthers → read only\n```\n\n---\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe symbolic string is composed of **ten character positions**:\n\n1. **Position 1 — File type:** A single character indicating the kind of object. In the example, `-` denotes a regular file.\n2. **Positions 2–4 — Owner permissions:** The first permission triplet, here `rwx`.\n3. **Positions 5–7 — Group permissions:** The second permission triplet, here `r-x`.\n4. **Positions 8–10 — Others permissions:** The third permission triplet, here `r--`.\n\nWithin each triplet, the three slots are always ordered **read, write, execute**. A letter (`r`, `w`, `x`) means the permission is **granted**; a hyphen (`-`) means the permission is **absent**. Thus `r-x` means read and execute are granted while write is denied.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example 1 — The given string `-rwxr-xr--`:**\n\n| Class  | Triplet | Read | Write | Execute |\n|--------|---------|------|-------|---------|\n| Owner  | `rwx`   | Yes  | Yes   | Yes     |\n| Group  | `r-x`   | Yes  | No    | Yes     |\n| Others | `r--`   | Yes  | No    | No      |\n\n**Example 2 — A read-only file for everyone:**\n\n```text\n-r--r--r--\n```\n\nOwner, Group, and Others all have read only.\n\n**Example 3 — Full access for owner, nothing for others:**\n\n```text\n-rwx------\n```\n\nOwner has read + write + execute; Group and Others have no permissions.\n\n---\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nEach permission triplet can be treated as a **3-bit binary number**, where each bit corresponds to a permission:\n\n$$\n\\text{bit} =\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & \\text{if the permission is granted (r, w, or x)} \\\\\n0 & \\text{if the permission is absent (-)}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe ordering within a triplet is **read (most significant), write, execute (least significant)**. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\text{Value} = r \\cdot 2^2 + w \\cdot 2^1 + x \\cdot 2^0\n$$\n\nFor the given string:\n\n- Owner `rwx` → $1\\cdot4 + 1\\cdot2 + 1\\cdot1 = 7$\n- Group `r-x` → $1\\cdot4 + 0\\cdot2 + 1\\cdot1 = 5$\n- Others `r--` → $1\\cdot4 + 0\\cdot2 + 0\\cdot1 = 4$\n\nThis shows how the symbolic representation maps directly onto the numeric (octal) representation.\n\n---\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Symbolic Representation | Numeric (Octal) Representation |\n|--------|-------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| Form | `-rwxr-xr--` | Derived from the same bits |\n| Readability | Human-readable, shows each permission explicitly | Compact, requires decoding |\n| Owner triplet | `rwx` | 7 |\n| Group triplet | `r-x` | 5 |\n| Others triplet | `r--` | 4 |\n| File type | Shown by first character (`-`) | Not shown in the triplet values |\n\nThe symbolic form makes the **presence or absence** of each individual permission immediately visible, whereas the numeric form compresses each triplet into a single value.\n\n---\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Misreading the hyphen:** A `-` inside a triplet means the permission is **denied**, not that the slot is empty or irrelevant.\n- **Confusing the leading `-`:** The first character is the **file type**, not a permission. It must not be counted as part of the owner's triplet.\n- **Assuming fixed letters:** The positions are always `r`, `w`, `x` in that order; a missing permission appears as `-` in its correct slot (e.g., `r-x`, not `rx-`).\n- **Mixing up classes:** The order is always **Owner → Group → Others**; reversing them produces a wrong interpretation.\n\n---\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the **ten-position layout**: 1 file-type character + 3 triplets of 3 characters each.\n- Remember the triplet order is always **read, write, execute**.\n- Practice translating between the symbolic string and the per-class meaning table.\n- For the given string `-rwxr-xr--`, be ready to state: Owner = read + write + execute, Group = read + execute, Others = read only.\n- Watch the leading character carefully — it tells you the **file type**, and only the remaining nine characters describe permissions.",
