          "id": "chgrp",
          "title": "chgrp",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 4 Concept 16",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on chgrp: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## chgrp\n\nChanges the group ownership.\n\n```sh\nchgrp developers file.txt\n```\n\nFor a directory recursively:\n\n```sh\nchgrp -R developers project/\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**`chgrp`** (change group) is a standard POSIX/Unix command-line utility used to **change the group ownership** of one or more files or directories. Every file in a Unix-like filesystem carries ownership metadata consisting of a **user owner (UID)** and a **group owner (GID)**, alongside a set of permission bits. The `chgrp` command modifies only the **group owner** component of this metadata, leaving the user owner and permission bits untouched.\n\nIt is the group-specific counterpart to `chown` (which changes user ownership, and can also change group) and `chmod` (which changes permission bits). Because group ownership governs which users — beyond the owner — can access a file according to the group permission triad (`rwx`), `chgrp` is a fundamental administrative tool for collaborative file sharing and access control.\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Metadata lookup** — The command resolves the target group name (e.g., `developers`) to a numeric **GID** using the system group database (`/etc/group` and related sources such as NSS).\n2. **Permission check** — The kernel verifies the invoking user has the right to change the group. Typically the user must **own the file** and be a **member of the target group**; the superuser (`root`) may change group ownership arbitrarily.\n3. **Inode update** — On success, the kernel writes the new GID into the file's **inode** metadata. The file's data blocks are not modified.\n4. **Recursion (optional)** — With `-R`, the operation is applied to the directory and all of its contents, descending through the tree.\n\nBecause group ownership is stored in the inode, the change is atomic per file and immediately reflected in `ls -l` output (the third field shows the group).\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Change group of a single file\n\n```sh\nchgrp developers file.txt\n```\n\nAfter this, `ls -l file.txt` shows `developers` in the group column.\n\n### Change group recursively for a directory tree\n\n```sh\nchgrp -R developers project/\n```\n\nEvery file and subdirectory under `project/` is reassigned to the `developers` group.\n\n### Verify the change\n\n```sh\nls -l file.txt\n# -rw-r--r-- 1 alice developers 1024 Jan 1 12:00 file.txt\n```\n\n### Typical collaborative workflow\n\nA shared project directory is assigned to a team group so that all members of `developers` can read/write according to the group permission bits:\n\n```sh\nchgrp -R developers project/\nchmod -R g+rw project/\n```\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nOwnership and permissions can be modeled as a tuple per inode:\n\n$$\n\\text{inode} = (\\text{UID},\\ \\text{GID},\\ \\text{mode})\n$$\n\n`chgrp` performs the transformation:\n\n$$\n(\\text{UID},\\ \\text{GID}_{\\text{old}},\\ \\text{mode}) \\xrightarrow{\\text{chgrp}} (\\text{UID},\\ \\text{GID}_{\\text{new}},\\ \\text{mode})\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{GID}_{\\text{new}}$ is the GID resolved from the supplied group name. Note that **UID and mode are invariant** under `chgrp`.\n\nFor a recursive operation over a set of paths $P = \\{p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_n\\}$:\n\n$$\n\\forall p_i \\in P:\\ \\text{GID}(p_i) \\leftarrow \\text{GID}_{\\text{new}}\n$$\n\nThe permission triad for a file is conventionally encoded as three octal digits:\n\n$$\n\\text{mode} = (u\\,g\\,o)_8\n$$\n\nwhere the **group digit** $g$ controls access for members of the file's group — the very group that `chgrp` sets.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Command | Changes | Typical Use |\n|---------|---------|-------------|\n| `chgrp` | Group owner (GID) only | Assign files to a team group |\n| `chown` | User owner (UID), optionally group | Transfer file ownership |\n| `chmod` | Permission bits (mode) | Grant/revoke read/write/execute |\n| `chgrp -R` | Group owner across a tree | Bulk reassignment for directories |\n\n| Aspect | `chgrp` | `chown` |\n|--------|---------|---------|\n| Scope | Group only | User (and optionally group) |\n| Syntax | `chgrp GROUP FILE` | `chown USER[:GROUP] FILE` |\n| Recursive flag | `-R` | `-R` |\n| Privilege needed | Ownership + group membership (or root) | Usually root |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting `-R`** — Running `chgrp developers project/` without `-R` changes only the directory itself, not its contents.\n- **Assuming `chgrp` changes permissions** — It changes **group ownership only**; access rights still depend on the mode bits set by `chmod`.\n- **Using a non-existent group** — Supplying a group name not present in the group database causes an error.\n- **Permission errors** — A non-root user cannot change a file's group unless they own the file and belong to the target group.\n- **Confusing group vs. user ownership** — `chgrp` never alters the user owner; use `chown` for that.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the mnemonic: **`chgrp` = change group**; **`chown` = change owner**; **`chmod` = change mode**.\n- The recursive flag is **`-R`** (capital R) — a frequent exam distractor.\n- `chgrp` modifies the **GID in the inode**; it does **not** touch UID or permission bits.\n- Know the canonical example: `chgrp developers file.txt` and `chgrp -R developers project/`.\n- Be ready to state the privilege rule: owner + group membership, or root.\n- Link `chgrp` conceptually to the **group permission triad** in `ls -l` output.",
