          "id": "handling-user-accounts",
          "title": "Handling User Accounts",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 4 Concept 27",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Handling User Accounts: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Handling User Accounts\n\nUNIX is a multi-user operating system.\n\nEach user generally has:\n\n- Username\n- UID\n- Primary GID\n- Home directory\n- Login shell\n- Password/authentication information\n\n```text\nUSER ACCOUNT\n                         │\n        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐\n        ▼                ▼                ▼\n     Username           UID              GID\n        │                                 │\n        ▼                                 ▼\n    Login name                         Group\n        │\n        ├──────── Home Directory\n        │\n        └──────── Login Shell\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**Handling User Accounts** in UNIX refers to the administrative process of creating, configuring, maintaining, and removing user identities on a multi-user operating system. Because UNIX is fundamentally designed as a **multi-user operating system**, it must distinguish between different users and control their access to system resources. Each user is represented by a **user account**, which is a structured record containing identity and authentication information.\n\nA user account binds together several attributes:\n\n- **Username** — the human-readable login name.\n- **UID (User IDentifier)** — a numeric identifier used internally by the kernel.\n- **Primary GID (Group IDentifier)** — the numeric identifier of the user's primary group.\n- **Home directory** — the user's personal working directory.\n- **Login shell** — the program started when the user logs in.\n- **Password/authentication information** — credentials used to verify identity.\n\nThese attributes collectively allow the system to authenticate users, authorize actions, and isolate resources.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe principle behind handling user accounts rests on **identification, authentication, and authorization**:\n\n1. **Identification** — The user presents a **username** at login.\n2. **Authentication** — The system verifies the supplied **password/authentication information**.\n3. **Mapping** — The system maps the username to a numeric **UID** and **primary GID**.\n4. **Session setup** — The system assigns the **home directory** and starts the **login shell**.\n5. **Authorization** — All subsequent file and process operations are checked against the UID/GID.\n\nThe kernel works primarily with numeric **UID** and **GID** values, while humans interact with the textual **username** and group name. The account record therefore acts as the bridge between human-friendly names and kernel-level numeric identifiers.\n\n```text\nLogin name  ──►  UID / GID  ──►  Kernel authorization\n   (human)         (numeric)          (enforcement)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Account Attribute Set\n\nA typical account entry conceptually contains:\n\n| Attribute | Purpose |\n|-----------|---------|\n| Username | Login name presented by the user |\n| UID | Numeric identity used by the kernel |\n| Primary GID | Numeric group identity |\n| Home directory | Personal working directory |\n| Login shell | Program launched at login |\n| Password/authentication information | Credential verification |\n\n### Example 2: Login Flow\n\n```text\n1. User enters username        →  Identification\n2. User enters password        →  Authentication\n3. System resolves UID + GID   →  Mapping\n4. Home directory is set       →  Session setup\n5. Login shell is started      →  Session start\n```\n\n### Example 3: Multi-User Isolation\n\nBecause each user has a distinct **UID** and **primary GID**, two users on the same UNIX system remain logically separated even though they share the same machine. The **home directory** provides each user a private workspace, and the **login shell** defines their interactive environment.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe account model can be expressed as a mapping from a username to a tuple of attributes:\n\n$$\n\\text{Account} = \\langle \\text{Username},\\ \\text{UID},\\ \\text{Primary GID},\\ \\text{Home},\\ \\text{Shell},\\ \\text{Auth} \\rangle\n$$\n\nThe resolution of a login name to numeric identity is a function:\n\n$$\nf : \\text{Username} \\rightarrow (\\text{UID},\\ \\text{Primary GID})\n$$\n\nAuthorization for a resource $R$ can be modeled as a predicate over the user's identifiers:\n\n$$\n\\text{Allowed}(\\text{UID},\\ \\text{GID},\\ R) \\in \\{\\text{true},\\ \\text{false}\\}\n$$\n\nThese formulations emphasize that the **username** is a label, while the **UID** and **GID** are the operative numeric keys used for enforcement.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Attribute | Human-Readable Form | Numeric/Kernel Form | Role |\n|-----------|--------------------|--------------------|------|\n| Identity | Username | UID | Distinguishes users |\n| Group | Group name | Primary GID | Distinguishes groups |\n| Workspace | Home directory path | — | Personal storage |\n| Environment | Login shell | — | Interactive session |\n| Security | Password/authentication info | — | Verifies identity |\n\n| Aspect | Username | UID |\n|--------|----------|-----|\n| Audience | Humans | Kernel |\n| Type | Text | Numeric |\n| Stability | Can be changed | Internal identifier |\n| Usage | Login | Authorization |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing username with UID** — the username is a human label; the UID is the numeric identifier the kernel uses.\n- **Forgetting the primary GID** — every user account includes a primary group, not just a UID.\n- **Overlooking the home directory** — it is a core account attribute, not an optional extra.\n- **Ignoring the login shell** — the shell is part of the account definition and determines the login environment.\n- **Treating authentication as separate** — password/authentication information is an integral part of the account record.\n- **Assuming single-user behavior** — UNIX is a multi-user operating system, so account handling is fundamental, not incidental.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Memorize the **six core attributes**: Username, UID, Primary GID, Home directory, Login shell, Password/authentication information.\n- Remember the key statement: **UNIX is a multi-user operating system** — this is the foundation for why accounts exist.\n- Be ready to **draw the account diagram** showing Username, UID, and GID branching to Login name, Group, Home Directory, and Login Shell.\n- Distinguish clearly between **Username (human-readable)** and **UID (numeric)**.\n- Link **Primary GID** to the concept of **Group**.\n- Use the tuple formulation $\\langle \\text{Username}, \\text{UID}, \\text{Primary GID}, \\text{Home}, \\text{Shell}, \\text{Auth} \\rangle$ to recall all attributes in one line.\n- Practice explaining the **login flow**: identification → authentication → mapping → session setup → shell start.",
