          "id": "etcshadow",
          "title": "/etc/shadow",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 4 Concept 35",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on /etc/shadow: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## /etc/shadow\n\n/etc/shadow stores password-related authentication information and password-aging data on systems using traditional shadow passwords.\n\nIt is normally restricted to privileged access.\n\n```text\n/etc/passwd\n      │\n      ├── User identity information\n      │\n      ▼\n/etc/shadow\n      │\n      └── Password authentication data\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**/etc/shadow** is a system file on Unix-like operating systems that holds the **encrypted password hashes** and **password-aging (expiry) metadata** for user accounts. It is the companion file to `/etc/passwd`, which retains user identity information (username, UID, GID, home directory, login shell) but no longer stores the password hash itself.\n\nThe mechanism is known as **shadow passwords**: the sensitive authentication data is \"shadowed\" — moved out of the world-readable `/etc/passwd` into a separate file whose read permissions are restricted to privileged access. This separation allows `/etc/passwd` to remain readable by all users (needed for username-to-UID resolution) while the password hash remains protected.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Separation of data:** `/etc/passwd` contains user identity information. `/etc/shadow` contains password authentication data.\n2. **Restricted access:** Because `/etc/shadow` holds password hashes, it is normally restricted to privileged access — ordinary users cannot read it.\n3. **Lookup by username:** Each line in `/etc/shadow` corresponds to a user, matched to `/etc/passwd` by the **username** field.\n4. **Authentication flow:** When a user logs in, the authentication subsystem reads the stored password hash and aging data from `/etc/shadow` (via privileged access) and compares it against the hash of the supplied password.\n5. **Aging enforcement:** The password-aging fields in `/etc/shadow` are consulted to determine whether the account's password is still valid, must be changed, or has expired.\n\n```text\n/etc/passwd\n      │\n      ├── User identity information\n      │\n      ▼\n/etc/shadow\n      │\n      └── Password authentication data\n```\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Relationship between the two files\n\nThe diagram below shows the conceptual split: identity data lives in `/etc/passwd`, while authentication data lives in `/etc/shadow`.\n\n```text\n/etc/passwd\n      │\n      ├── User identity information\n      │\n      ▼\n/etc/shadow\n      │\n      └── Password authentication data\n```\n\n### Example 2 — Login scenario (conceptual)\n\n- A user supplies a username and password at login.\n- The system resolves the username using `/etc/passwd` (user identity information).\n- The system then consults `/etc/shadow` for the password authentication data.\n- The supplied password is hashed and compared with the stored hash.\n- Password-aging data in `/etc/shadow` is checked to confirm the password is still valid.\n\n### Example 3 — Why the split matters\n\nIf password hashes remained in `/etc/passwd`, any user able to read that file could obtain the hashes. By moving password authentication data into `/etc/shadow` and restricting it to privileged access, the hashes are shielded from unprivileged reads.\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe core authentication check can be expressed as a comparison between the hash of the supplied password and the stored hash:\n\n$$\n\\text{stored\\_hash} \\stackrel{?}{=} H(\\text{supplied\\_password})\n$$\n\nwhere $H$ is the password-hashing function used by the system.\n\nAuthentication succeeds when:\n\n$$\nH(\\text{supplied\\_password}) = \\text{stored\\_hash}\n$$\n\nPassword-aging validity can be expressed conceptually as a check that the current time falls within the permitted window defined by the aging data:\n\n$$\n\\text{valid} \\iff \\text{aging constraints satisfied}\n$$\n\n> Note: The specific hashing algorithm and aging field semantics are not specified in the source notes; only the general principle of comparing a hash and consulting aging data is stated.\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | /etc/passwd | /etc/shadow |\n|---|---|---|\n| Primary content | User identity information | Password authentication data |\n| Password hash stored? | No (in shadow-password systems) | Yes |\n| Aging data stored? | No | Yes (password-aging data) |\n| Access restriction | Readable (identity resolution) | Normally restricted to privileged access |\n| Role in authentication | Provides identity lookup | Provides password hash and aging data |\n| Relationship | Companion file | Companion file |\n\nThe table contrasts the two files based on the division of responsibilities described in the notes: identity versus authentication.\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming the password hash is in `/etc/passwd`.** In systems using traditional shadow passwords, the password authentication data resides in `/etc/shadow`, not `/etc/passwd`.\n- **Treating `/etc/shadow` as world-readable.** It is normally restricted to privileged access; expecting ordinary users to read it is incorrect.\n- **Confusing identity with authentication.** `/etc/passwd` supplies user identity information; `/etc/shadow` supplies password authentication data.\n- **Ignoring password-aging data.** `/etc/shadow` is not only about the password hash — it also carries password-aging data used to enforce password lifetime rules.\n- **Forgetting the username linkage.** Entries in `/etc/shadow` are associated with users by username, matching the corresponding entry in `/etc/passwd`.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Memorize the one-line definition:** `/etc/shadow` stores password-related authentication information and password-aging data on systems using traditional shadow passwords.\n- **Remember the access rule:** it is normally restricted to privileged access.\n- **Reproduce the diagram** to show the split between user identity information (`/etc/passwd`) and password authentication data (`/etc/shadow`).\n- **Use the phrase \"traditional shadow passwords\"** when describing the context in which `/etc/shadow` is used.\n- **Contrast the two files** in any answer: identity vs. authentication, readable vs. privileged access.\n- **Mention both responsibilities** of `/etc/shadow`: password-related authentication information **and** password-aging data.",
