          "id": "types-of-shells",
          "title": "Types of Shells",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 5 Concept 2",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Types of Shells: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Types of Shells\n\n### Academic Definition\n\nA **shell** is a command-line interpreter that provides a user interface for UNIX/Linux operating systems. It acts as an intermediary between the user and the operating system kernel, reading commands from the user, interpreting them, and requesting the kernel to execute the corresponding programs. Shells are both an interactive command environment and a scripting language, allowing users to automate sequences of commands.\n\nThe term \"shell\" is used because it represents the outermost layer of the operating system — a \"shell\" around the kernel. Different shells have evolved over time, each offering distinct features, syntax, and capabilities. The most common UNIX/Linux shells include **sh** (Bourne Shell), **csh** (C Shell), **ksh** (Korn Shell), and **bash** (Bourne Again Shell), along with others such as **zsh**, **fish**, and **tcsh**.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe shell operates in a read-evaluate-execute loop:\n\n1. **Read** — The shell displays a prompt and reads input from the user (interactive mode) or from a script file (non-interactive mode).\n2. **Parse** — The input is parsed into commands, arguments, redirections, pipes, and control operators.\n3. **Interpret** — The shell determines whether the command is a built-in (executed internally) or an external program (located via the `PATH` variable).\n4. **Execute** — For external commands, the shell forks a child process and uses `exec` to run the program; for built-ins, it executes directly.\n5. **Return** — The shell waits for the command to finish, captures the exit status, and returns to the prompt for the next command.\n\nEach shell implements this loop with its own syntax and feature set, which is why scripts written for one shell may not run identically in another.\n\n### Shell Hierarchy\n\n```text\nUNIX SHELLS\n                         │\n        ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐\n        ▼                ▼                ▼\n       sh               csh              ksh\n        │                │                │\n     Bourne           C Shell          Korn Shell\n        │\n        ▼\n      bash\n   Bourne Again Shell\n```\n\nOther shells include:\n\n```text\nzsh\nfish\ntcsh\n```\n\nBash is one of the most commonly used shells on Linux systems.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**1. Bourne Shell (sh)** — The original UNIX shell developed by Stephen Bourne at Bell Labs. It is the standard shell for scripting and is available on virtually all UNIX systems.\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\necho \"Hello from the Bourne Shell\"\n```\n\n**2. C Shell (csh)** — Developed by Bill Joy, it introduced C-like syntax, command history, and aliases. It is popular among users familiar with the C programming language.\n\n```csh\n#!/bin/csh\necho \"Hello from the C Shell\"\n```\n\n**3. Korn Shell (ksh)** — Developed by David Korn, it combines Bourne shell compatibility with C shell features such as history and aliases.\n\n```ksh\n#!/bin/ksh\necho \"Hello from the Korn Shell\"\n```\n\n**4. Bourne Again Shell (bash)** — A superset of `sh` developed for the GNU Project. It is the default shell on most Linux distributions and supports scripting, history, tab completion, and more.\n\n```bash\n#!/bin/bash\necho \"Hello from Bash\"\n```\n\n**5. Other shells** — `zsh` (Z Shell) offers advanced completion and customization; `fish` (Friendly Interactive Shell) emphasizes user-friendliness; `tcsh` is an enhanced version of `csh`.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Shell | Full Name | Developer | Key Features | Typical Use |\n|-------|-----------|-----------|--------------|-------------|\n| sh | Bourne Shell | Stephen Bourne | Standard scripting, POSIX-compliant | Portable scripts |\n| csh | C Shell | Bill Joy | C-like syntax, history, aliases | Interactive use |\n| ksh | Korn Shell | David Korn | sh compatibility + csh features | Scripting & interactive |\n| bash | Bourne Again Shell | GNU Project | Superset of sh, history, completion | Default on Linux |\n| zsh | Z Shell | — | Advanced completion, customization | Power users |\n| fish | Friendly Interactive Shell | — | User-friendly, modern syntax | Beginners |\n| tcsh | TENEX C Shell | — | Enhanced csh | csh users |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- Assuming all shells are interchangeable — scripts written for `bash` may fail in `sh` or `csh` due to syntax differences.\n- Forgetting the shebang line (`#!/bin/bash`) in scripts, causing them to run under an unintended shell.\n- Confusing the shell with the terminal emulator — the terminal is the window; the shell is the interpreter running inside it.\n- Using `csh`-specific syntax (e.g., `setenv`) in a Bourne-compatible shell, or vice versa.\n- Overlooking that `bash` is a superset of `sh` but not identical to it in POSIX mode.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the hierarchy: `sh` → `bash`; `csh` → `tcsh`; `ksh` is a hybrid.\n- Know that **bash** is the default and most commonly used shell on Linux systems.\n- Be able to match each shell to its developer: Bourne (sh), Joy (csh), Korn (ksh).\n- Recall that `sh` is the POSIX standard shell for portability.\n- Distinguish between the shell (interpreter) and the terminal (interface).\n- Expect questions on the shebang line and its role in script execution.",
