          "id": "shell-script-structure",
          "title": "Shell Script Structure",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 5 Concept 4",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Shell Script Structure: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Shell Script Structure\n\nA basic shell script can contain:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\n\necho \"Hello World\"\n```\n\nThe first line is called the shebang or hashbang.\n\n```text\n#!/bin/bash\n    │\n    └── Specifies the interpreter\n```\n\nExecution:\n\n```text\nShell Script\n     │\n     ▼\n#!/bin/bash\n     │\n     ▼\nBash Interpreter\n     │\n     ▼\nCommands\n     │\n     ▼\nOutput\n```\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **shell script** is a text file containing a sequence of commands written for a **command-line interpreter** (the shell). The shell reads the file line by line and executes each command. The **first line** of a well-formed script is the **shebang** (also called **hashbang**), written as `#!` followed by the path to the interpreter — in this concept, `#!/bin/bash`. This line tells the operating system which interpreter should process the remainder of the file. The script body then contains the actual commands, such as `echo \"Hello World\"`, which are executed in order by that interpreter.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe structure of a shell script follows a simple, linear principle:\n\n1. **Shebang line** — `#!/bin/bash` is the very first line. The `#!` prefix is a special marker recognized by the operating system, and `/bin/bash` specifies the interpreter.\n2. **Interpreter invocation** — When the script is run, the system launches the interpreter named in the shebang (here, the **Bash Interpreter**).\n3. **Command execution** — The interpreter reads and executes the commands that follow, one after another.\n4. **Output** — The result of the commands (for example, the text printed by `echo`) is produced as output.\n\nThis flow is captured in the execution diagram:\n\n```text\nShell Script\n     │\n     ▼\n#!/bin/bash\n     │\n     ▼\nBash Interpreter\n     │\n     ▼\nCommands\n     │\n     ▼\nOutput\n```\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Minimal script\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\n\necho \"Hello World\"\n```\n\n- Line 1: `#!/bin/bash` — the **shebang**, specifying the Bash interpreter.\n- Line 2: blank line (optional spacing).\n- Line 3: `echo \"Hello World\"` — the command executed by the interpreter.\n\n### Structure breakdown\n\n```text\n#!/bin/bash\n    │\n    └── Specifies the interpreter\n```\n\nThe shebang is not a command in the usual sense; it is a directive to the system identifying **which interpreter** should run the script.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe structure can be expressed as an ordered sequence of components:\n\n$$\n\\text{Script} = \\langle \\text{Shebang},\\ \\text{Commands} \\rangle\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\text{Shebang} = \\texttt{\\#!} \\parallel \\text{InterpreterPath}\n$$\n\nand for the example:\n\n$$\n\\text{InterpreterPath} = \\texttt{/bin/bash}\n$$\n\nExecution can be modeled as a mapping from script to output:\n\n$$\n\\text{Execute}(\\text{Script}) \\rightarrow \\text{Output}\n$$\n\nwith the interpreter applied to the command list:\n\n$$\n\\text{Output} = \\text{Bash}(\\text{Commands})\n$$\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Component | Role | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shebang / Hashbang | Specifies the interpreter | `#!/bin/bash` |\n| Interpreter | Executes the commands | Bash Interpreter |\n| Commands | Actions to perform | `echo \"Hello World\"` |\n| Output | Result produced | `Hello World` |\n\n| Term | Also Known As | Symbol |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shebang | Hashbang | `#!` |\n| Interpreter path | Shell path | `/bin/bash` |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Omitting the shebang** — without `#!/bin/bash`, the system may not know which interpreter to use.\n- **Placing the shebang on a line other than the first** — the shebang must be the **first line** of the script to be recognized.\n- **Confusing the shebang with a comment** — although it begins with `#`, the shebang is a special directive, not an ordinary comment.\n- **Incorrect interpreter path** — the path after `#!` must point to the intended interpreter (e.g., `/bin/bash`).\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the exact spelling: **shebang** or **hashbang**.\n- Memorize the shebang form: `#!/bin/bash`.\n- Be able to state that the shebang **specifies the interpreter**.\n- Recall the execution order: **Shell Script → `#!/bin/bash` → Bash Interpreter → Commands → Output**.\n- A minimal script consists of the **shebang** plus **commands** (e.g., `echo \"Hello World\"`).",
