          "id": "file-tests-in-shell-scripts",
          "title": "File Tests in Shell Scripts",
          "subtitle": "CA454 Unit 5 Concept 56",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on File Tests in Shell Scripts: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## File Tests in Shell Scripts\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**File tests** in shell scripting are conditional operators used within the `test` command (commonly invoked through the `[ ... ]` construct) to examine the properties and status of files and directories on the filesystem. They allow a script to determine whether a path exists, what type of object it refers to (regular file, directory, etc.), and other attributes, before the script proceeds to act on that path. File tests form the backbone of defensive shell programming, enabling scripts to **validate input before performing operations**.\n\n### Working Principle\n\nThe shell evaluates the expression inside `[ ... ]` and returns an exit status: `0` (true) if the condition holds, and non-zero (false) otherwise. This exit status drives the `if`/`then`/`else` control flow.\n\n- `[ -f \"$1\" ]` — tests whether the path stored in `$1` is a **regular file** that exists.\n- `[ -d \"$1\" ]` — tests whether the path stored in `$1` is a **directory** that exists.\n\nThe quoted variable `\"$1\"` is passed as the operand to the test operator. Quoting ensures the value is treated as a single argument even if it contains spaces or is empty.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example 1 — Regular file existence check:**\n\n```sh\nif [ -f \"$1\" ]\nthen\n    echo \"Regular file exists\"\nelse\n    echo \"File does not exist\"\nfi\n```\n\nHere the script inspects the first positional parameter (`$1`). If it names an existing regular file, the message `\"Regular file exists\"` is printed; otherwise the script reports `\"File does not exist\"`.\n\n**Example 2 — Directory test:**\n\n```sh\nif [ -d \"$1\" ]\nthen\n    echo \"Directory exists\"\nfi\n```\n\nThis variant checks whether `$1` refers to an existing directory. If so, it prints `\"Directory exists\"`. Note that there is no `else` branch here — the script simply takes no action when the condition is false.\n\n**Example 3 — Combined validation (conceptual use):**\n\nBecause file tests are useful for **writing scripts that validate input before performing operations**, a script might first confirm a path is a regular file with `-f` and only then read or process it, or confirm a path is a directory with `-d` before listing its contents.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe test construct can be viewed as a predicate function mapping a path to a boolean outcome:\n\n$$\n\\text{isRegularFile}(p) =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{true} & \\text{if } p \\text{ is an existing regular file} \\\\\n\\text{false} & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{isDirectory}(p) =\n\\begin{cases}\n\\text{true} & \\text{if } p \\text{ is an existing directory} \\\\\n\\text{false} & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe shell's exit status encodes the boolean:\n\n$$\n\\text{exitStatus} =\n\\begin{cases}\n0 & \\text{if the test is true} \\\\\n\\text{non-zero} & \\text{if the test is false}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | `-f` (Regular File Test) | `-d` (Directory Test) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | Checks for an existing regular file | Checks for an existing directory |\n| Typical usage | `[ -f \"$1\" ]` | `[ -d \"$1\" ]` |\n| Example message on success | `\"Regular file exists\"` | `\"Directory exists\"` |\n| Example message on failure | `\"File does not exist\"` | (no `else` branch in the example) |\n| Role in validation | Confirms a file is present before file operations | Confirms a directory is present before directory operations |\n\nBoth tests share the same structure — a bracketed condition, a quoted operand, and an `if` block — differing only in the operator and the object type they detect.\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting to quote the variable.** Using `[ -f $1 ]` instead of `[ -f \"$1\" ]` can break when the argument contains spaces or is empty. The notes consistently use `\"$1\"`.\n- **Confusing file type tests.** `-f` matches only regular files; it will not succeed for a directory. Use `-d` for directories, as shown in the directory test example.\n- **Assuming a missing `else` means failure is handled.** In the directory example, there is no `else` branch, so nothing is reported when the directory does not exist. Scripts that need feedback on failure must add an `else`.\n- **Skipping validation entirely.** The notes emphasize that these tests are useful for **validating input before performing operations** — omitting them risks acting on nonexistent or wrong-type paths.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the two core operators demonstrated: **`-f`** for regular files and **`-d`** for directories.\n- Be able to reproduce the exact example snippets, including the messages `\"Regular file exists\"`, `\"File does not exist\"`, and `\"Directory exists\"`.\n- Note the structural pattern: `if [ <test> \"$1\" ]` / `then` / `echo ...` / `else` / `echo ...` / `fi`.\n- Emphasize the purpose stated in the notes: file tests are used for **writing scripts that validate input before performing operations**.\n- Always quote the operand (`\"$1\"`) in your answers to demonstrate correct, safe shell scripting practice.",
